11:28:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it feels like that should be true, but actually—it's not what happens in practice, and this is a real point of confusion (and even legal dispute). ## Check the Actual Boxes Apple, Samsung, and most major manufacturers deliberately **avoid the word "waterproof"** in their official specs. Look at an iPhone box or spec sheet—it says "water resistant" and gives the IP rating (like IP68) with the tested conditions. Apple's own support page explicitly states: > "Splash, water, and dust resistant... iPhone was tested... in controlled laboratory conditions... Splash, water, and dust resistance are not permanent conditions." ## Why "Waterproof" Gets Used Anyway If you're seeing "waterproof" on a box, it's likely from: - **Third-party retailers or case makers** (not the phone manufacturer) using loose marketing language - **Regional packaging** with less regulatory oversight - **Older colloquial use**—the word "waterproof" existed before precise IP testing standards did, and habit persists in casual speech even among companies ## This Has Literally Been Litigated Samsung got sued (and settled) in Australia and elsewhere over Galaxy phone ads implying full waterproofing, because customers' phones died after water exposure despite IP68 ratings. The Australian ACCC took action specifically because "waterproof" implied a guarantee the phones didn't meet. This is exactly *why* companies now lean hard on "water **resistant**" plus IP codes in official material—to avoid that legal exposure. ## The Actual Standard The IP rating system itself, which is the industry/engineering standard these claims are based on, has no rating called "waterproof." It only has resistance levels tied to specific tested depths and times. There is no code for "water can never get in under any circumstance." So if you're going by what's on an official spec sheet from Apple/Samsung/Google, you won't find the word "waterproof" at all—it's water*resistant*, with an asterisk that is the whole IP rating.
