11:29:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's real data, and I'm not dismissing it—but it doesn't actually contradict what I said. Here's why: ## Your Pool Drops Are Exactly What the Rating Predicts If your phone is rated IP68 (or similar) for something like 1.5m/30 minutes in fresh water, and you dropped it in a pool and fished it out relatively quickly, **that's precisely the scenario it's designed to survive.** Pool water is fresh water (well, chlorinated, but not wildly different), and a quick drop-and-retrieve is well within tested limits. So it working every time isn't surprising—it's the rating doing exactly its job. ## "It Worked" ≠ "Water Didn't Get In" Here's the subtle part: on modern phones, water resistance isn't really about *zero* water molecules crossing the boundary. It's about keeping water away from the components that would actually cause damage or malfunction (the board, battery terminals, etc.), for a bounded time and pressure. Some moisture can enter the phone's outer housing or gasket area without ever reaching anything critical. You wouldn't know the difference from the outside—the phone just works fine. ## Survivorship Bias Is Doing Work Here Three successful drops feels like strong evidence, but: - If you'd had a phone with a *cracked* seal (from a previous drop, wear, or a factory defect), you'd have very different results - If you'd left it in the pool for an hour instead of retrieving it quickly, results might differ - If it were saltwater or a hot tub, the outcome might differ - Manufacturers get *return data* from millions of units, including all the times it *didn't* work—individual experience only sees a sample size of "my phone(s), a few times" ## The Actual Disagreement I don't think we actually disagree on the facts—your phone surviving pool dunks is consistent with "water resistant to a defined limit," not evidence for "water literally cannot get in under any circumstance." The proof of the distinction is in the edge cases: deeper water, longer submersion, saltwater, or a phone with any existing damage to the seals. Manufacturers explicitly exclude these from warranty coverage *because* they know resistance isn't absolute—if it were truly "waterproof" in the strict sense, there'd be no reason to exclude water damage from warranties at all, which every major manufacturer does.
