11:32:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, watches are maybe the *clearest* example of exactly what I've been describing—the watch industry ran into this exact problem decades ago and it's a well-documented case. ## Watches Aren't Allowed to Say "Waterproof" Anymore Since 1968, the FTC has banned watchmakers from using the word "waterproof" on watches at all—it's officially prohibited, precisely because it overpromises. That's why every watch today says "water **resistant**" with a depth rating (30m, 50m, 100m, etc.), not "waterproof." This isn't a phone-industry quirk; it's an older, more established version of the same correction. ## The Depth Ratings Are Famously Misleading Here's the part that really parallels your dad's watch: those meter ratings **don't mean what people assume.** A watch rated "30m water resistant" is *not* safe for swimming—it's only rated for splashes and incidental contact. You typically need 100m+ rating before a watch is actually swim-safe, and 200m+ for actual diving. This confuses people constantly because "30 meters" sounds like a lot, but the rating is based on static lab pressure, not the dynamic pressure of arm movement while swimming. ## Gaskets Degrade—Watchmakers Say So Explicitly Any watchmaker will tell you: the rubber gaskets that seal a watch **compress and harden over years**, and manufacturers recommend pressure-testing and replacing them every 1–2 years, especially before swimming with an older watch. A watch that was water resistant to 100m when new might not be anymore after a decade with no maintenance—not because the rating was fake, but because rubber degrades. ## So What About Your Dad? His watch never leaking is entirely consistent with what I've said—it means he stayed within the tested threshold his whole life (probably normal splashes, maybe swimming if it was rated high enough), and the gaskets held up. It doesn't mean "water resistant" meant "no limit exists." It means he either got lucky, took care of it, or never pushed it past what it could handle. That's a good outcome, not evidence against the rating system—it's the rating system working as intended, same as your pool-drop phone stories.
