11:32:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, actually—and you've just landed on the right instinct. "Unbreakable" glass (like Gorilla Glass, Ceramic Shield, etc.) is exactly the same pattern: it means "resistant to typical drops from typical heights onto typical surfaces," not "cannot be broken." Drop your phone from shoulder height onto concrete versus carpet, and you'll get very different results despite it being the same "unbreakable" glass. People shatter these screens all the time, obviously—so the pattern-matching instinct you just had is correct. ## But This Isn't "Trust Nothing" Here's the actual useful takeaway, not a spiral into total skepticism: **absolute-sounding words on consumer products almost always mean "very resistant, tested to a specific threshold"—not literally 100% invincible.** Once you know that pattern, you don't have to re-litigate it for every product. You can just assume it going forward: - "Waterproof" → resistant to X depth/time - "Unbreakable" → resistant to normal drops - "Fireproof" (safes) → resistant for a rated number of minutes at a rated temperature - "Shatterproof" → resistant to typical impacts, not literally never breaks - "Stainless" steel → resists staining, but can still stain/rust under the right conditions ## Where It Actually Stops It stops at genuinely absolute physical/chemical facts, which are rare in consumer marketing: something is either radioactive or not, contains gluten or doesn't, is a specific voltage or isn't. Those are binary. But almost any word describing **resistance to physical damage or environmental stress** (water, drops, fire, scratches, impact) is describing a threshold, not an absolute, because physics doesn't offer absolutes for those things—more force/pressure/time can always overcome more resistance. ## A Simple Rule of Thumb If a marketing claim is about **surviving some kind of stress test** (water, drops, heat, impact), assume "resistant up to a point" and look for the actual rating/number if it matters to you (IP rating, drop height in the fine print, etc.). If the claim is about **composition or identity** (contains X, is made of Y), that's more likely to be a hard fact. You're not being paranoid by asking "where does this stop"—you've actually just figured out the general rule that explains all of these at once, instead of needing me to walk you through them one by one.
