11:35:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good catch—that's a sharper challenge than the others, and I should be honest: I overstated that distinction earlier. Let me correct it rather than defend it. ## You're Right That Those Aren't Clean Absolutes Either "Gluten-free" isn't actually zero gluten molecules—legally it means under 20 parts per million (in the US/EU), because you can't test for or guarantee literal zero at the molecular level. "Sterile" in medical contexts doesn't mean zero microorganisms exist ever—it's defined by a "Sterility Assurance Level," typically a 1-in-a-million probability threshold, not an absolute guarantee. So I was wrong to hold those up as clean, threshold-free absolutes in contrast to "waterproof." They're both threshold claims too. That's a fair correction on your part. ## So What's the Actual Difference? Now that I'm being more careful, the real distinction isn't "absolute vs. resistant"—it's about **what kind of threshold, and why it exists**: - **Gluten-free / sterile**: the threshold is about *detection limits and measurement*, at a fixed point in time. Once verified under 20ppm, that's the composition of the product—it doesn't change based on external conditions. You're testing "what's in this thing right now." - **Waterproof / bulletproof**: the threshold is about *resisting an external force that can always be increased*. Water pressure can always go up (more depth). Time can always be extended. Impact energy can always be higher. There's no version of "waterproof" that isn't implicitly "resistant up to X pressure/time," because you can always apply more of the stressor—there's no equivalent move for gluten (you can't "apply more gluten-detecting force" to a sample and expect the answer to change). ## The Honest Update So the fairer statement isn't "some claims are absolute, others are resistant"—it's that **all these claims are threshold-based, but some thresholds are about static composition (measurable, fixed) and others are about dynamic resistance to escalating force (inherently open-ended).** "Waterproof" falls in the second category, same as bulletproof and fireproof, which is why none of them can ever mean zero-exceptions the way you want—not because of a double standard, but because the type of claim doesn't allow for a stress-free "done" state the way a composition test does. You were right to push on that—thanks for catching the inconsistency.
