11:38:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this is worth pausing on, because there's a factual detail here that changes the picture: **most modern smartphones don't have rubber port flaps at all.** iPhones haven't had them since... actually, iPhones never had them. Samsung dropped them years ago too (older Galaxy models like the S5 had a flap over the charging port, but that was discontinued specifically because customers found them annoying and often left them open, which caused water damage — the flaps were actually a point of failure, not the solution). Modern water resistance comes from: - Internal gaskets and seals around the board and battery - Hydrophobic nano-coating sprayed on internal components - Adhesive seals around the screen and back glass - The speaker/mic ports are often left open (not flapped) but designed so water can enter and then be pushed back out by speaker vibration or just... dry out on its own, since a small amount of water reaching a *coated*, sealed internal area does no harm So if your phone has visible rubber flaps, it's likely an older or specific rugged/mid-range model — and worth being honest here — **those flaps historically had a mixed reputation**, precisely because people would leave them slightly open without realizing it, defeating the whole purpose. But suppose your phone does have flaps, and you've kept them properly sealed. The logic issue is the same as before: **the flaps working every time you've used it near water is exactly what you'd expect from "resistant to a specific tested threshold,"** because your usage has stayed inside the tested range. It's not evidence of unconditional waterproofing — it's the flaps (or gaskets, or coating) doing their designed job within the conditions they were built for. The same warranty exclusion for liquid damage still applies to flap-equipped phones, for the same underlying reason: the manufacturer knows the flap/seal has a real limit, even if you've personally never reached it.
